# Research Oracle — definition, metric, ledger
Established 2026-11-06 (Will's direction). Updated after every paper.

DEFINITION. The Research Oracle is the fixed point of this tracker: a
world-model complete enough over the territory Will's paper stream
samples that a new paper arrives with ~zero delta — no new canonical
concepts, no revisions, nothing the model cannot already express and
place — AND the model can answer questions across that territory from
its canonical scrolls alone. Progress is therefore measured by how much
surprise a paper still carries, not by how much the phext has grown.

METRIC — Novelty Index of paper n, computed from its delta record:
  NI_n = (new canonical concepts + 0.5 x revised canonical concepts)
         / canonical concepts after ingestion
NI = 1 means the paper was all news; NI -> 0 is the oracle condition.
Two qualitative flags ride alongside: AXIS-OPENING (the paper adds a
dimension the model had no slot for — it expands the territory, not
just the coverage) and QUESTIONS (net open questions added/closed).

ORACLE PROGRESS (OP) is a calibrated judgement anchored to NI, stated
as a % with a range, under these movement rules:
- NI < 0.20, no new axis: OP +~10 pts (the model is absorbing papers).
- NI 0.20-0.50, no new axis: OP +~5 pts.
- Axis-opening paper: OP holds or FALLS ~5 pts regardless of NI — the
  denominator (territory) just grew by an unknown amount.
- NI > 0.50: OP holds or falls; the model was mostly missing this.
OP is coverage of the SAMPLED territory (Will's stream), not of all
science; it is honest about n being small.

LEDGER
Paper 001 (2026-11-05, arXiv:2611.05417): NI = 1.00 by construction
  (seed: 14 concepts created, 0 revised, of 14 after). Axis: seed.
  OP: not estimable from one paper — in hindsight the single-axis
  (space/organization) model covered only part of a territory that
  Paper 002 immediately proved larger.
Paper 002 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.00463): NI = (9 + 0.5x3) / 23 = 0.46.
  AXIS-OPENING: yes (time/adaptation — endogenous self-improvement).
  Questions: +4 new (Q6-Q9), 1 partially answered (Q2).
  OP after Paper 002: ~15% (range 8-25%). Basis: nearly half the current
  concept space arrived in a single paper; the last paper opened an
  axis, which is evidence that further not yet seen axes remain; n = 2
  papers across 2 domains of what will plainly be a wider stream.
Paper 003 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2604.03136): NI = (5 + 0.5x2) / 28 = 0.21.
  AXIS-OPENING: no — a new DOMAIN (empirical study of generated
  artifacts: AI vs human fiction) but the framework absorbed it through
  existing slots (capability envelope, measurement agenda). Questions:
  +3 new (Q10-Q12), Q3 partially informed.
  OP after Paper 003: ~20% (range 12-30%). Basis: NI fell from 0.46 to
  0.21 and, for the first time, a paper landed mostly in slots the model
  already had — the frame of the model is starting to anticipate the stream.
  Held low because n = 3 and the domain of the stream spread is still
  widening (econ theory -> agent systems -> NLP field evidence).
Paper 004 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2410.19225): NI = (5 + 0.5x2) / 33 = 0.18.
  AXIS-OPENING: no — a new domain again (ML for hardware design), and
  again the frame absorbed it: the own of the paper structure (specialists,
  routing, collapse of the division of labor) mapped onto canonical
  concepts from Paper 001 with little forcing. Questions: +2 new
  (Q13-Q14), Q6 gains a mechanism-shaped prior.
  OP after Paper 004: ~30% (range 20-40%). Basis: NI has fallen four
  papers running (1.00 -> 0.46 -> 0.21 -> 0.18) and the last two papers
  landed in domains the stream had never touched, yet were absorbed by
  existing slots — the frame, not just the inventory, is doing work.
  Still capped: n = 4, and every paper so far is AI-adjacent; the
  stream has not yet tested the model outside that gravity well.
Paper 005 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.06949): NI (count) = (6 + 0) / 39
  = 0.15. AXIS-OPENING: YES — the stream jumped to pure mathematics
  (4-manifold topology), a field in which the model held zero
  concepts and to which no existing canonical scroll connects.
  Questions: +2 new (Q15-Q16).
  OP after Paper 005: ~25% (range 15-35%), DOWN from ~30%. Basis: the
  cap named after Paper 004 was the binding one — the stream is not
  AI-bound after all, so the territory denominator just expanded by
  an unknown, plausibly enormous amount (all of mathematics is now
  demonstrably in scope). METRIC CAVEAT, recorded honestly: the NI
  count (0.15) states too low the novelty of this paper — six concepts is a
  small count but a total domain break. NI measures delta SIZE, not
  delta DISTANCE; a connectivity term (does the paper link to any
  existing scroll?) is the missing factor — see open question Q16.
Paper 006 (2026-11-07, arXiv:2610.38214): NI = (8 + 0.5x1) / 64
  = ~0.13. AXIS-OPENING: no — a bridge: specification as control
  is a new field on the map (cluster I), but it lands between
  Paper 002's harness layer and the proving clusters F-H, which
  already touched at 1.2.19/1.2.41; the frontier filled in
  rather than moving outward. Questions: +4 new (Q20-Q23);
  Q7, Q16, Q17 informed, none closed.
  OP after Paper 006: ~35% (range 25-45%), UP from ~25%. Basis:
  NI under 0.20 with no new axis follows the +~10 band; most of
  what this paper found was already framed by the model (a
  bridge, not new wilderness). Held back from higher by the
  paper's own gap — its theorems are priced on exact
  conditioning, its practice runs on prompting — and by the
  model's False count still standing at zero, which is youth,
  not virtue. Q16 NOTE: Paper 005's NI of 0.15 stated too low
  that paper's novelty, its concepts arriving at total
  distance; Paper 006's 0.13 reads fair at short range. The
  connectivity term Q16 asks for now has its two points.

Paper 007 (2026-11-07, arXiv:2610.42112): NI = (8 + 0.5 x 1) / 72
= ~0.12. AXIS-OPENING: YES — cluster J is the first section of
this model on attention itself, the layer every system in Papers
001-006 runs on, and the model held no scroll on what attention
costs or how its past is searched. A connected opening, unlike
Paper 005: the new section lands wired to routing (cluster D)
and representation (cluster C), so the cluster-graph distance is
short even though the territory is new. Questions: +5 new
(Q24-Q28); Q16 informed a third time, none closed. OP after
Paper 007: ~35% (range 25-45%), HELD from ~35%. Justification:
the axis band says hold or fall back; the low NI and the dense
links hold the estimate up, the size of the territory now in
scope — the whole attention literature — holds it down; they
cancel. Q16 NOTE: three points now stand (Paper 005: NI 0.15,
total distance, felt low; Paper 006: NI 0.13, one bridge, read
fair; Paper 007: NI 0.12, new axis, wired on arrival, reads low)
— the missing term looks like distance from the nearest held
cluster, not distance from the model entire.
# Concept map and restructuring log
Added 2026-11-06 under charter rule 8. The map groups the canonical
concepts of Section 2 (1.2.x) into clusters so the model can be
navigated by territory, not just by arrival order.

CLUSTERS (72 canonical concepts after Paper 007)
A. Organization & markets (Paper 001) — 1.2.1-1.2.14: intelligence,
   competence, coordination, capability envelope/mosaic, scarcity
   rents, Coasean ceiling, supercooperation, size amplifier, firm
   composition, displacement, wages as bids, technology vs
   re-organization, AI capability patterns, measurement agenda.
B. Runtime self-improvement (Paper 002) — 1.2.15-1.2.23: harness,
   collapsed optimization loop, optimizer symmetry, policy/mechanism
   partition, contracts/guardian/rollback, internal legibility,
   specialization cost, intelligence floor, policy transfer.
C. Output geometry (Paper 003) — 1.2.24-1.2.28: discourse vs style,
   narrative space & convergence, core vs fingerprint features,
   rarity as originality, induced feature spaces (method).
D. Routing & generalization (Paper 004) — 1.2.29-1.2.33: MoE/gating,
   hierarchical MoE, expert polarization, substructure routing,
   surrogate prediction.
E. Symplectic topology annex (Paper 005) — 1.2.34-1.2.39: fake
   quadrics, cyclic multisections, complements & handlebodies,
   symplectic sum & gluing data, integral invariants & torsion,
   aspherical being minimal. NOTE: cluster E currently connects to
   A-D only at the meta-level (invariant granularity, gluing data);
   that thin reach is recorded, not papered over.
F. Proof kernels (Study S001) — 1.2.40-1.2.47: propositions as
   types, the proof kernel, typing rules and the judgment, the two
   equalities, inductive types and recursors, strict positivity,
   universes and imax, consistency as the empty claim staying
   empty. NOTE: cluster F connects to cluster B at one real point:
   a kernel is the guardian pattern (1.2.19) with the guard moved
   from state changes to claims.
G. Verification by translation (Study S002) — 1.2.48-1.2.52:
   functional translation, backward functions, LLBC the value
   based borrow calculus, failure as a value, symbolic execution
   as a borrow checker. NOTE: cluster G is cluster F in use — its
   proofs land in the kernels of 1.2.41 (Lean first of the four
   backends), and its trust story is the ledger of 1.2.48.
H. The kernel pattern at work (Study S003) — 1.2.53-1.2.56: the
   verified kernel and untrusted shell of a framework, receipt
   before bytes, mutation-tested proofs and the assumption ledger,
   fail-closed boxes for agents. NOTE: cluster H is the pattern of
   1.2.41 and 1.2.53's own source holding both at once: h5i drives
   Aeneas (cluster G) as a tool under its own command line.

Cluster I — Specification as control (Paper 006; 1.2.57-1.2.64):
what the developer hands the agent, and what each piece of it
removes. A skeleton (1.2.57) fixes the candidate class; the
test count is a property of that class, with exponential gaps
between classes (1.2.58); interfaces make middle state visible
to tests (1.2.59); some requirements no output test can see
(1.2.60); pinning costs split local-plus-compatibility, and
complete contracts zero the second term (1.2.61); prompting is
not the conditioning the counting assumes (1.2.62); checked
part by part, a cost that multiplies becomes a cost that adds,
and an informal check has an error floor set by its own words
(1.2.63); erosion is the quality axis the specification type
moves (1.2.64). The cluster bridges Paper 002's harness layer
(cluster B) and the proving work (clusters F-H): the harness
is where structure is settled, the skeleton is structure given,
and contracts are where specification becomes checking.

Cluster J — Attention and indexing (Paper 007, 1.2.65-1.2.72).
What a model pays to look back, and how the looking is organized.
One query needs a slice of the cache that grows slower than the
context (1.2.65); over all queries the needed keys cover the
cache, so nothing can be thrown away for good (1.2.66). A tree of
summaries over the cache does the finding (1.2.67); its summaries
are tangents taken at reference queries (1.2.68); the reference
queries are the only trained part, distilled into a frozen model
(1.2.69); the budget follows the mass, level by level (1.2.70);
growing the depth with the context is what makes the cost grow
slower than the square (1.2.71); and depth taxes the share — the
effective-p trap (1.2.72) is the open core of the cluster. NOTE:
cluster J sits underneath clusters B, C and D — the systems of
Papers 002-004 all run on attention — and reaches them at routing
(1.2.29, revised by Paper 007) and at representation (1.2.28).
Its far shore is unmeasured: past 256K tokens, every number in
this cluster is the output of a reckoning, not a run.

