# Study S002 — source record: Aeneas (a toolchain)
Source: github.com/AeneasVerif/aeneas — 'Aeneas: Rust
Verification by Functional Translation', the toolchain of Son Ho,
Jonathan Protzenko and others (Microsoft Research and Inria
among their homes). Open code (Apache 2.0), written in OCaml 5,
built with Dune. Studied 2026-11-06: the README and the
documentation in the repo read in full; the layout of the code
and the tests measured; the two papers the repo points to read
at the level of their claims (Ho and Protzenko, ICFP 2022,
arXiv:2206.07185; Ho, Fromherz and Protzenko, ICFP 2024,
arXiv:2404.02680). This is a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not
a paper of the stream.
The pipeline, in the words of the repo: Rust is read by Charon (a
front end of the Rust compiler, its own repo; Aeneas pins the
exact Charon it works with in a file named charon-pin) into LLBC
(1.2.50); Aeneas carries LLBC into pure functional code for one
of four proof assistants: Lean, F*, Coq, or HOL4 — Lean being, by
the words of the repo, 'the most mature and actively maintained'
of the backends, the others held thinner. The deployment the repo
names: the verification of ML-KEM (the NIST standard FIPS 203,
the Kyber design) as it stands in Rust inside SymCrypt, the
cryptography library of Microsoft.
# Study S002 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
ADDS (5 canonical concepts, cluster G, concept map 1.1.5):
verification by translation (1.2.48); backward functions
(1.2.49); LLBC (1.2.50); failure as a value (1.2.51); symbolic
execution as a borrow checker (1.2.52).
CHANGES TO CANONICAL SCROLLS: none. CONFIRMS: the floor of
cluster F — the proofs of Aeneas land in the kernels of 1.2.41,
which is the trust the whole toolchain comes down to (ledger at
1.2.48).
CHECKS RUN (from the study, measured 2026-11-06, not taken from
the repo): the translator is 77,012 lines of OCaml, tests counted
in; its heart, the symbolic interpreter, is 25,584; the Lean
backend library is 275 files and 49,782 lines, the HOL4 one 25
files, the F* and Coq ones one file each; the test inputs are 128
in number, with 7 files marked as known failures — expected
errors, kept in the open; the newest commit in the copy studied
is dated the day of the study (2026-11-07 by the clock of the
repo) and is work on the standard library models.
TENSIONS AND LIMITS (from the study, in the words of the repo):
safe Rust only, and not all of it — no code the repo calls
unsafe, no running in parallel; two standing limits (a return
inside nested loops and breaks to an outer loop are not carried
yet; a general type filled in with a mutable borrow is not
carried yet); outside code is not carried at all — it is met by
hand-written models, which are trusted (1.2.48); unsafe code and
parallel code are a program of work to come, by way of separation
logic, not a thing the tool does today.
# Study S002 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Two links to the work of Will, stated as the reading
of Emise, not as the claims of the repo.
(1) SQ is Rust. Aeneas is, today, the standing road from Rust to
a kernel-checked proof (1.2.48) — the road Q17 asks about. Its
price is written in its own ledger: the translator and the front
end are trusted, so a proof by this road is a proof about the
translation of SQ, held to SQ itself by two paper proofs and by
the care of the pin. For a small set of claims the rest stands
on, that may be the right price; the ledger lets it be priced
with open eyes.
(2) The backward function (1.2.49) is a phext-shaped idea wearing
Rust clothes: a value travels with the function that puts it
back. State that leaves a boundary carrying its own way home is
the shape of every honest handoff in the Exocortex — a scroll is
only as good as the update that returns it.
What the study does NOT give: proofs about unsafe Rust, about
code that runs in parallel, or about the parts of SQ that call
outside code with no model written — and nothing that makes the
proof work itself cheap: the specifications are written by hand,
loop by loop.
Open question added: Q18 — for a claim the rest stands on, which
road prices better: the translation of the shipped Rust (Aeneas,
trusted translator), or a small model built by hand in the type
theory itself (the world of MiniLean4 — a hand-built model, and
the one who builds it trusted)?
