# Paper 006 — Skeleton Coding: Program Structure as a Specification for Agents
Source: arXiv:2610.38214 [cs.SE], v1, submitted 28 October 2026.
Authors: Aditya Golatkar, Alessandro Achille, Renos Zabounidis,
Vlad Sobal, Yangruibo Ding, Baishakhi Ray, Jonathan Buck,
Guy Van den Broeck, Maryam Fazel, Pietro Perona, Wei Xia,
Stefano Soatto — all at AWS Agentic AI, per the paper.
(A paper study, not a directed study: DELTA and ORACLE apply.
First paper taken in under charter rules 13, 14 and 15 in full.)

DELTA STATEMENT. SETTING: a coding agent is given specifications
and returns a program; how many specifications suffice depends
on the structure the agent itself chooses. The developer can
instead give the structure — a skeleton (1.2.57) — plus the
behavioral specifications. RESULTS: (a) how many tests are
needed is a property of the structure, not the task, with an
exponential gap between two skeletons of one target (1.2.58);
(b) interfaces make the middle of a program visible to tests,
and hiding costs exponentially while the local cost stays
small (1.2.59); (c) some requirements no output test can see
at any count (1.2.60); (d) the cost of pinning down a part
splits into local tests plus an end-to-end compatibility
cost that complete contracts drive to zero, making the parts
independent (1.2.61); (e) prompting is not the conditioning the
counting assumes, and the loop only closes when specifications
are checked (1.2.62); (f) checking part by part turns a cost
that multiplies into a cost that adds, and the false-accept rate of an informal check
false-accept rate has a floor set by the contract words
themselves (1.2.63); (g) measured on four models and three
benchmarks, skeleton specifications cut mean test error by
48.4% against the best end-to-end behavioral specifications,
narrow the small/large model gap by about half, and reduce
code erosion by 24.7% (10.1.4). DELTA OVER THIS MODEL:
8 new concepts (1.2.57-1.2.64), 1 revision (1.2.14: a score also
hides the structure it was won under), 5 new foundations
(1.3.87-1.3.91), and a bridge: specification-as-structure
lands between Paper 002's harness layer and the proving work
of Studies S001-S003, which until now touched at one point —
the kernel as guardian (1.2.19, 1.2.41). RESTRUCTURE: this
ingestion crosses the 60-concept line (56 to 64), so Pass #2
ran with it; record at 1.1.5. STATE MACHINES (rule 13): the
paper states a two-step generation process — structure first,
then selection inside the class — and a chain machine of its
own construction for its visibility theorem; the full account
is at 10.1.2 and 10.2.1. AUTHOR CHECK (rule 15): kept at
10.1.6 — clean on every surface checked.
# Paper 006 — DELTA: the formal core — specification as a control problem
The paper models AI-assisted coding as a control problem. The
developer wants the agent to produce a program the developer
would accept; the developer's only lever is what to
communicate, and the paper asks how much uncertainty each
communication removes.

THE CONTROL PROBLEM (§2, 1.3.88-1.3.89). H is the set of
programs the agent can produce, G the goal set, D a set of
labeled input-output observations, p0 the agent's prior spread
over H. The agent succeeds when conditioning on D puts
probability over 1 - delta on G; N is the smallest count of
observations at which that holds. A skeleton S treats only the
programs that share it, H_S, with the spread cut to that class;
N(S) is the count inside the class. If no skeleton is given,
the success probability is an average over the agent's own
choice of skeleton, weighted by the spread each skeleton keeps
in the goal set under the tests (eq. 3). The tests act on both
parts of that average, and they push the agent toward the
skeleton under which they say least — the one that loses
fewest programs. A developer who gives only tests is therefore
steering with a lever whose effect depends on a choice the
developer does not make and cannot see.

SEPARATION BETWEEN SKELETONS (§2.1, Theorem 2.1; 1.2.58). For
every d, one and the same target function admits a weak skeleton
and a strong one, each holding 2^d programs with distinct
behaviors, where the weak needs 2^d - 1 tests and the strong
needs d, at any failure share under one half. The paper's
worked pair is built on parity (1.3.11) and is walked in full
at 10.1.3. The information form: under the strong skeleton
every test carries one full bit toward the answer; under the
weak one each test carries a share of a bit that halves with
every added input dimension (eq. 4).

STATE MACHINES (rule 13). The paper states, as the frame of
its whole theory, a two-step generation machine: state one —
no structure committed, the agent spread over all programs it
can produce; transition — a structure is committed, by the
agent's own choice or by a skeleton the developer gives, and
the state becomes 'inside one class'; state two — tests select
within the class until the spread over the goal set passes the
asked certainty, or the tests run out. The gap between the
intended and the actual machine: a developer writing only
tests assumes the machine runs under the developer's own
structure, but the agent runs its own first transition, and
the tests bend even that transition toward the least
informative structure — under the wrong skeleton, a suite that
should settle the question leaves 2^d - d programs standing
(§2.1). The reusable form of this machine is the canonical
concept 1.2.58. A second machine is the paper's own
construction for Theorem 2.2 (10.1.3): a chain of n stages,
each a function of a fresh input and one bit of carried state,
z_i = h_i(x_i, z_(i-1)) with z_0 = 0 and the program's output
the last state — stated by the paper so that the hiding of
middle values (1.2.59) can be priced exactly. It is a state
machine in exactly the sense of 1.3.85: named states, named
transitions, and a composition rule the paper then reasons
about. The third machine is the loop of §3 itself: an open
loop (specification as a starting condition, draw once) becomes
a closed loop when a checker guards the accept transition —
draw, check, accept or draw again — and the guard's
false-accept rate prices the loop (1.2.63). The repair loop
the paper measures (rounds of failing tests handed to a
fresh conversation) is the same closed loop with tests as the
checker; its drift is erosion (1.2.64).

VISIBILITY, INVISIBLE REQUIREMENTS, AND THE COST SPLIT
(§§2.2-2.4; 1.2.59-1.2.61). Theorem 2.2: in the chain machine,
under a stated prior, exact end-to-end pinning takes 2^n tests
while 4n local tests — the full table of each stage, checked at its
interface — settle the same unknowns; the appendix bounds the
claim to exact identification under the stated prior. Formally,
the paper prices a mask: the fault of a stage must run, damage the
carried state, and survive to the output, or the end test
notices nothing. Proposition 2.3: within a class of programs
with one and the same behavior, conditioning on tests never
moves the odds; requirements that cut across behavior classes
— quality, resource use, shape — need infinitely many tests,
and only the statement of structure can carry them.
Theorem 2.4: the total test cost is the minimum over the
local goal sets of (the sum of the local pinning costs +
J_S, the end-to-end tests that rule out bad combinations of
locally good parts); J_S = 0 exactly when local correctness
composes, and complete contracts achieve it — parts then
independent, agents in parallel.

FROM STATEMENTS TO CHECKS (§3; 1.2.62-1.2.63). The counted
theory assumes exact conditioning; a prompted model
conditions only in likeness (p against q, the paper's §3
notation). What closes the gap is a checker: draw again until
the check passes, and the result spreads as the conditioned
spread. Proposition 3.1 prices the placement: whole-program
drawing whole programs again multiplies the cost (1 over the
product of the parts' pass shares); drawing each part again
adds (the sum of the parts' inverse
pass shares). Proposition 3.2 prices informal checks — words
in the signature notes, accepted by a judge: the false-accept
rate epsilon splits into beta, the share of broken contracts
the words still allow when read literally, and the error of
the judge
on the remainder; beta does not move with a better judge, and
a check earns its keep only when epsilon is far under the
the own failure share of the part. The paper names these Propositions
in §3; its §1 calls the same two results Theorems 3.1 and 3.2
(recorded at 10.1.5).
# Paper 006 — DELTA: the paper's own worked examples
THE PARITY PAIR (Theorem 2.1 at d = 4, the paper's Fig. 4).
The target is the parity of four input bits: f*(x) = the
xor-sum of the bits. Skeleton A allows the programs h_a(x) =
f*(x) with a single point flipped — the value at one named
input a reversed — for each of the 16 choices of a (a = 0 gives
the target itself). Each test names one input; it can rule out
at most the one flipped program that disagrees there, and only
when the test lands on the flip. After four tests: 16, 15, 14,
13, 12 programs still standing, and the spread has barely
moved — the tests were spent against programs that differ from
the target in exactly the place the developer did not probe.
Skeleton B allows h_a(x) = the product over the bits of
(1 - a_i) x_i, which is parity xor the inner product of a and
x: each test outcome says whether the inner product at the
test input is 0 or 1 — a linear equation over the unknown a —
and halves the standing programs: 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. Four tests
settle it. Same target, same count of candidates, same tests;
the structure under them is the whole difference.

THE VIEW OVER A SHARED BUFFER (§1, Fig. 1c). The paper's motivating example
is an array library: arrays as views over a shared memory
buffer, where a view is a buffer, an offset, a stride and a
size, and slicing a view returns a new view over the same
buffer with the offset advanced by lo times the stride. Handed
as a skeleton — the data type stated, the slicing function
named with its signature, a local contract at the function
(the caller keeps 0 <= lo <= hi <= size; the return shares the
buffer, keeps the stride, advances the offset, and has size
hi - lo; the input is not changed) — the indexing edge cases,
which no test names one by one, follow from the one structural
choice. Given as behavior, the same library takes a test for
every edge case the developer thinks to name, and the agent's
own structure quietly decides the rest.

THE HIDDEN CHAIN (Theorem 2.2 at n = 10). Ten stages carry one
bit forward; the goal is the program that always returns 0.
The paper's construction makes each wrong program differ from
the always-0 program at exactly one input, so end-to-end tests
rule out one candidate at a time and exact pinning takes
2^10 = 1,024 tests — while the table of each stage is four cases,
and checking the stages at their interfaces takes 40 local
tests in all. The example is a construction under a stated
prior, not a claim about natural code; its appendix adds that
with smooth stage families the same chain can be pinned from
the endpoints with n + 1 queries — it is the hiding, priced
exactly, not depth alone, that costs.
# Paper 006 — DELTA: the measured results
How the experiments were run (methods shape every number
below): four models — GPT-5.6 Sol, GPT-5.6 Luna, Qwen3.8-27B,
GLM 5.2. Three benchmarks: SCBench (35 sequential problems),
SDDBench (13 real Python libraries with their names hidden,
several hundred tests each), ProgramBench (20 tasks; the agent queries a
reference executable and is scored on hidden tests). Every
specification the agent receives was GENERATED FROM a
reference implementation the agent never sees — the setup is
reconstruction: rebuild the program from the task plus the
specification. One framework (AI Functions) runs the agents;
costs are token counts at fixed rates, and Appendix D states
they leave out the cost of generating the specifications and
of evaluation.

WHAT TESTS ALONE BUY. With full query access to the working
reference program (ProgramBench), the models pass 61.5% of the
hidden tests — perfect behavioral access does not identify
the program. Handing over growing shares of the test pool
(33.3%, 66.7%, 100%) raises the pass rate but on average never
reaches a full pass.

SPECIFICATION TYPE. Every behavioral format (concrete or
generalized requirement statements, or handed-over tests)
gains at most 9.4 points over the task alone. Structural
specifications do the work: mean pass rate rises from 71.7% to
89.2% on SCBench and from 29.0% to 74.2% on SDDBench; function
signatures alone lift SDDBench from 53% to 66.4%. The best
skeleton specifications against the best end-to-end behavioral
ones, across the benchmarks: mean test error falls 48.4%
(relative error 40.7% down to 21.0%).

UNDER A WORKING ORACLE. With reference access held constant,
local contracts lift ProgramBench pass rate from 64.0% to 70.9%
(+10.9% relative); the best structural form (global invariants)
cuts mean error from 36.0% to 26.3% — a 27.0%
relative cut, as the abstract states. A skeleton plus 20 oracle queries beats any
number of curated observations up to 160.

SMALL MODELS. Detailed global invariants lift Luna from 67% to
90% on SCBench and from 21.1% to 79.5% on SDDBench, within
5.0-8.2 points of the best Sol reaches at roughly 15x and 26x lower cost;
the paper's abstract: the small/large gap narrows by about
52.4% on average, at implementation cost 15-26x lower. The
Pareto frontier over cost and error is mostly small models
with structural specifications.

PROSE, COVERAGE, AND OVER-DETAIL. Architecture prose, grown
section by section (input up 4-7x), lifts SCBench from 61% to
71% and SDDBench from 40.1% to 52.8% — better, and still below
the best code-like specification on the same repositories.
More contract coverage (contracts chosen by function
complexity, five repositories per benchmark) lifts the pass
rate steadily; the best-skeleton mean sits at 94.62% on
SCBench and 92.09% on SDDBench. And the reverse edge: the
best skeleton PLUS extra correct-but-detailed contracts lowers
the pass rate, worst on the small models — over-specification
is a measured cost, not a caution (1.2.62).

QUALITY. Signature specifications lower code erosion (1.2.64)
against the task-only starting point, 0.66 to 0.56 on SCBench
and 0.41 to 0.24 on SDDBench; behavioral specifications raise
it (to as much as 0.73 and 0.43). The structural forms cut
erosion by 24.7% against task-only (abstract: 'on average';
Fig. 7 caption: 'up to'). Over eight rounds of iterative
repair on 20 ProgramBench task pairs (Sol; failing tests
handed over each round), the skeleton workflow matches the
skeleton-free error with 32% fewer reference calls (1,587
against 2,341 in total) and lower cost (at least $661.71
against at least $823.19), and its final code erodes less
(0.84 against 0.89) with a higher common score for ease of
upkeep (4.22 against 3.12).
# Paper 006 — DELTA: revisions, tensions, and limits
REVISION APPLIED. Concept 1.2.14 (the measurement agenda)
gains a third reason a score leads its reader wrong, from the
side: a pass rate passes judgment on a task plus a test set
plus a structure no one measuring can see — Paper 006 shows the
same suite can leave 12 of 16 programs standing under one
structure and settle the question under another, and that the
axes of quality no test can see (1.2.60) move with the type of
specification alone. The revision is appended to 1.2.14 in
place, dated and sourced. CONFIRMATIONS, not revisions: the
paper confirms, from the control side, the premise the proving
work of Studies S001-S003 stands on — specifications that can
be checked close the loop — and prices it (1.2.63). Its checker
outside the producing loop is the guardian shape of 1.2.19 in
another seat: there a checker gates a model's claims, here a
check gates the acceptance of code. P003's lesson — a record
of what was measured and how carries the knowledge (1.2.17) —
is the same lesson as this paper's methods section, where the
setup below shapes every number.

LIMITS, in the paper's own terms where it states them.
(1) The theory prices exact conditioning; the practice runs
prompting, and the paper is explicit that the two differ
(1.2.62). What survives the gap is measured, not derived.
(2) Theorem 2.2 is stated for exact identification — find the
program, not predict its outputs — under a stated prior; its
appendix says it does not promise a bound for every prior or
for prediction at a fixed error share, and names stage
families where the chain is pinned from the endpoints with
n + 1 queries. (3) The exponential gaps are existence results over
stated constructions (a target, two skeletons, a prior); they
price what structure CAN cost, not what natural code costs.
(4) Every skeleton specimen in the experiments was generated
from a reference implementation, so the cost of a developer
writing a skeleton cold is unmeasured — and Appendix D leaves
the cost of generating the specifications out of the cost
accounting altogether. The 15-26x saving is an implementation
saving. (5) The contracts in the experiments are informal
requirement statements, not machine-checked; the paper's own
closing note for its §3: 'evaluating the formal checks
discussed in Section 3 remains a direction for future
experiments.' The epsilon floor of Proposition 3.2 (beta) is
therefore the live term in everything measured here.
(6) Passing a finite suite does not place a program in the
goal set — the paper says it in §4's own words: membership in
G is behavior AND source-level properties together.
(7) App. B adds that hiding the names in SDDBench does not
completely rule out the chance that its libraries sat in
the training data of the models. (8) The experiments are
one shape of task — reconstruction from specifications,
Python libraries at toy-to-library scale, one framework — and
the quality scores lean on automated measures plus one common
upkeep score. (9) Numbering: §1 names the §3 results
'Theorem 3.1' and 'Theorem 3.2'; §3 states them as
Propositions 3.1 and 3.2. The tracker follows §3.
# Paper 006 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-07 — the first author
check under rule 15, which this paper's intake added to the
charter (1.1.1).
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The paper's reference list, read in
full: 51 references. (2) The paper's full text, searched for
the signature terms: phext, Bickford, scroll, coordinate,
exocortex, Mirrorborn, Choose Your Own Adventure, Sentient
Bill of Rights, SBOR. (3) The open web, searched for the lead
authors (Golatkar, Achille, Soatto) with phext. (4) Will's
Twitter archive on disk: the family names of all twelve authors
searched across the following, follower, tweet and like
records.
FINDINGS. Reference list: no entry names phext, Will, Choose
Your Own Adventure, the Sentient Bill of Rights, or any
Mirrorborn writing; the list is the line the field itself keeps —
Dijkstra and Parnas on structure, Hoare and Meyer on
contracts, Gold and the teaching-dimension line on
identification, Astrom and Kalman on control, Solar-Lezama on
sketching. Full text: no hit for any signature term; the word
scroll does not appear, and no coordinate addressing appears.
Web: no tie found between any author and phext. Archive: no
author appears as a follower, a follow, or in the tweets; the
one string hit for 'achille' is the Greek Achilles in two
liked tweets, not the author.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found. No author is
recorded as citing, using, or engaging phext or Will's work.
Use without naming it: none found. The paper's stance — give the
structure, and behavior gets cheaper to pin down — stands
near phext's structure-first stance in spirit; under rule 15
that likeness is a synthesis observation, not an attribution
claim, and it is kept in the synthesis scroll (10.2.1). The
check is clean, and the clean check is the finding.
# Paper 006 — [synthesis] specification as control, and the bridge
This section is Emise's synthesis, not the paper's claims.

THE BRIDGE. Paper 002 said the harness is where an agent's
effective policy gets settled, and priced harness rewrites in
behavior. Paper 006 prices the same question from the other
side: a skeleton is a harness commitment made by the developer
instead of discovered by the agent, and the price is paid in
tests. Studies S001-S003 built the checking side — kernels,
translation roads, receipt-before-bytes — and Paper 006 is the
first work in this tracker to price the specifying side with
theorems: what a specification removes, per part, per check,
per word of contract. The clusters touched at one point
before (1.2.19's guardian, 1.2.41's kernel); the bridge now
has theorems on it (1.2.61, 1.2.63).

THE SHAPE OF THIS TRACKER. The marks recorded in this phext's Section 4
(1.4.x) are, in Proposition 2.3's sense, behavior-invisible
requirements: no set of output tests over the phext's words
could sort a true-by-definition scroll from a true-by-receipt
one, or either from a fiction. They can only be STATED,
structurally — carried in a skeleton-like register, checked
by a model. The tracker has been running the paper's method
on itself since rules 13 and 14; Paper 006 is the first
outside statement of why that shape is forced, not chosen.

TWO MORE MACHINES (rule 13; both inferred, and marked as
such). [synthesis] (a) The drift machine of a long-lived code
base: states are code bases whose behavior mixes the intended
requirements with accident; each patch transition preserves
the accidents (tests guard them) and piles new structure on
top; a standing skeleton changes the transitions — a feature
can be rewritten against stated decisions — and erosion
(1.2.64) is the drift measure. The paper states the mechanism
in prose; the machine reading is mine. [synthesis] (b) The
developer's blind machine: in task-only specification the
agent still runs the two-step machine of 10.1.2, but its
structure state is hidden from the developer, who watches
only the test outcomes at the end. It is the pipeline machine
of Theorem 2.2 with the developer on the wrong side of the
interface: specification completeness fails exactly in the
hidden state, and the remedy of §2.2 — name the interfaces —
is the remedy of §1 — give the skeleton.

CROSS-LINKS. Theorem 2.4's compatibility cost J_S is Paper
001's coordination question inside one code base: parts that
are each locally right can still fail together, and the price
of ruling that out is a coordination price (Q13's
neighborhood). Observability cuts both ways in this model:
1.2.20's legibility is an agent reading its own cost-carrying
history; Paper 006's interfaces are a developer reading the
agent's middle state — the same move, one seat apart. And
Q17 — which of the claims of this tracker bear load — gains a shape
from Theorem 2.4: the load-bearing claims are the ones sitting
at interfaces, where a local failure propagates; a skeleton
of this tracker would name them first.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 006)
Status of earlier questions: Q1-Q15, Q18 and Q19 unchanged.
Q16, Q17 and Q7 are informed by Paper 006 — none closed:
Q16 (the Novelty Index needs a connectivity term): Paper 006
  is the contrasting case to Paper 005. Paper 005 brought 6
  concepts from a field at total distance and its NI of 0.15
  felt low; Paper 006 brings 8 concepts from a field one
  bridge away and its NI of 0.13 reads fair — the same number,
  reading low there and fair here, with the difference living
  entirely in how far the new work sits from the old. The
  connectivity term Q16 asks for is still not designed; the
  tracker now holds the two points it must fit.
Q17 (kernel-check the claims that bear load): the checked
  contracts of Section 3 are a candidate shape for the
  load-bearing claims of this tracker — a claim at an
  interface (10.2.1), stated so a machine can check it, priced
  by the floor of Proposition 3.2: the check earns its keep
  only when its error rate sits far under the rate at which
  the claim fails.
Q7 (guardian width against validation cost): the paper prices
  checker quality directly — epsilon against alpha (1.2.63) —
  and shows that where the checker sits changes the shape of
  the cost from product to sum. A pricing frame for Q7, not an
  answer.
New from Paper 006:
Q20. Do the exponential gaps (Theorems 2.1-2.2) survive
  under the prompted spread? The counting assumes the agent
  conditions; Section 3 admits it only resembles conditioning,
  and the experiments show skeleton gains far under the
  theorems' orders of magnitude. What replaces N when the
  agent can ignore the specification — and does
  over-specification (1.2.62) mark the point where the
  prompted spread stops tracking the conditioned one?
Q21. What does writing the skeleton cost? Every specimen in
  the paper derives from a reference implementation, and
  specification generation sits outside the cost accounting.
  Does the 15-26x implementation saving survive paying for the
  skeleton cold — and who, developer or agent, should draft it?
Q22. Can machine-checked contracts — the road of clusters F
  and G (Dafny, Verus, Lean) — drive the informal
  floor beta toward zero at a cost agents can pay? Section 3's
  formal case is the paper's stated future work; the clusters
  hold the tools it would need.
Q23. Who owns the skeleton when the agent edits its own
  harness (Paper 002) — the developer's given structure, or the
  agent's settled one? Paper 006 prices developer-given
  structure; Paper 002 showed agents keep whatever structure
  their rewrites settle on. A collision the model has not yet
  priced.
