# Paper 007 — Subquadratic Attention with a Hierarchical Tree-Structured Indexer
Source: arXiv:2610.42112 [cs.CL], v2 (submitted 30 October 2026,
revised 2 November 2026).
Authors: Leon Lufkin and Cutter Dawes (marked equal first
authors), Alexander Speicher, Kamesh Krishnamurthy, Tomas
Figliolia, Beren Millidge — Zyphra Research, per the paper.
(A paper study, not a directed study: DELTA and ORACLE apply.)

DELTA STATEMENT, PLAINLY — Paper 007 taken the same day as Paper
006, 2026-11-07, under charter rules 1-15 including the author
check of rule 15, the state-machine review of rule 13, and the
epistemic pass of rule 14.

SETTING: Attention is the layer every model in this tracker runs
on, and the tracker held no scroll on it. Dense attention scores
each new token against every key in the cache, so its cost grows
with the square of the context. The known escapes divide: windows
and fixed-size states throw away reach; sparse methods keep reach
but the part that chooses which keys to score
either itself costs the square, or is trained in a way whose gain
shrinks as the context grows. The claim of ZHA: build an index of the
cache as a tree of compressed summaries (1.2.67), route each query
down the tree by mass (1.2.70), and let the depth grow with the
context; the total cost then grows slower than the square
(1.2.71), and a model already trained can be converted to it by a
short distillation that trains only the reference queries of the
router (1.2.69).

WHAT THE PAPER SHOWS (all of it run at or under 256K tokens;
anything past that is the reckoning of the paper's own speed
model, and is marked wherever this phext cites it):
(a) the premise, measured — the keys a query needs grow as a power
    law slower than the context (1.2.65), while over all future
    queries the needed keys cover the cache, so nothing can be
    thrown away for good (1.2.66);
(b) the construction — summaries as the tangents of the pull of a chunk
    at reference queries (1.2.68), one tree per head, built as
    tokens arrive, at a modeled upkeep near 1 percent of the
    attention cost;
(c) the conversion — reference queries distilled into two frozen
    30-35B models on one billion tokens: dense-level scores held
    on the reasoning, math, coding-agent and knowledge test sets, and on
    a 256K-token retrieval suite the deep router stands at 93.3
    against dense 96.0 while touching 1 to 6 percent of the keys
    (the measured results are at 11.1.4);
(d) the geometry study — 28 tree shapes: depth at a fixed total
    compression sustains retrieval at greater sparsity, and a
    four-parameter model of accuracy, fit at 64K and 128K,
    predicts the held-out 256K runs to a root-mean-square error
    of 1.44 points;
(e) the stated limit — the effective-p trap (1.2.72): at a fixed
    share per level the kept set must shrink toward a fixed size
    as depth grows, and the schedule that would save it is named,
    not built.

DELTA OVER THIS MODEL: 8 new canonical concepts, 1.2.65-1.2.72,
placed as the FIRST cluster on attention and indexing — cluster J —
a new section of the model, connected on arrival to routing
(1.2.29, revised by this paper) and to representation (1.2.28,
linked, not revised). 8 foundations (1.3.92-1.3.99) and 2 defined
words (1.3.100, 1.3.101) added. Questions: Q16 informed a third
time; Q24-Q28 new. The narrative goes to v9; the registry is drawn
a third time (1.4.1-1.4.6). No other canonical scroll is rewritten or
stated again.

RESTRUCTURE: none triggered. The pass counter stands at 72 of 80
concepts; the next rule-14 pass is due at Paper 010 or 80 concepts,
whichever comes first — 8 concepts away on the second count.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13; the review is at 11.1.2 and
11.2.1): the paper states its router as a machine — a descent
whose state is the level and the set of nodes still open — and its
tree builder as a second machine, filling buffers and closing
summaries on a doubling clock. The inferred machines of Emise,
marked [synthesis]: the governor the paper needs and does not
build, and the two ways of keeping an index, built or learned.

AUTHOR CHECK (charter rule 15): CLEAN, recorded at 11.1.6 — the paper is not
recorded as friendly to phext, and no use was found that this
check could see. CHANGELOG: v2 (2 Nov 2026) from v1 (30 Oct 2026); the
tracker takes v2.
# Paper 007 — DELTA: the formal core — an index for the cache, and where the slower-than-square cost comes from

THE PREMISE, AS MEASURED (Sections II-A and II-B; concepts 1.2.65
and 1.2.66). Two facts about trained attention carry the whole
construction. First: the keys any one query needs grow as a power
law in the context length, exponent under 1 — at the largest
shares plotted, the needed set at a billion tokens is a small
share of the cache. Second: the union of the needed sets over
future queries covers the cache. Together the two fix the design
space before any machinery is chosen: keep everything, retrieve
per query.

THE INDEX (Section III-A; 1.2.67, 1.2.68). The cache is cut into
chunks of C keys. At every node the tree holds R compressed keys:
for reference query r, the key the chunk would have if its keys
were averaged with the weights of r's own attention over the
chunk, plus a bias term drawn from the spread of those weights.
The pair — compressed key, bias — is the tangent of 1.3.97 made
concrete: to score a node under a new query, take the largest,
over r, of the score of the query against the compressed key,
plus the bias. Children merge into a parent by running the same averaging
on the compressed keys of the children. Where position rotations
are in play, the merge is corrected for the rotation of the chunk,
and the result is a lower bound on the true chunk score, not the
score itself — the paper flags this as the one place where its
scores are a bound on the quantity they stand for. Two standing
exceptions ride outside the index: the first key of the cache,
which draws a large share in trained models, and the chunk now
being written are always attended directly.

THE TREE KEEPS ITSELF [state machine M2] (Section III-A). Tokens
arrive one by one. A buffer fills; when C new keys stand in a row,
their level-1 summaries close; for each higher level, a summary
closes every C-to-that-level tokens. The tree therefore grows on a
doubling clock — most steps close nothing, rare steps close a deep
node — and the roofline reckoning of Appendix D puts the upkeep
near 1 percent of the total attention cost. The state of the
machine is the stack of open buffers, one per level.

THE TRAINING [state machine M3] (Section III-B; 1.2.69). Stage
one: freeze the model. Stage two: run text; at each level, match
the estimated shares of the tree over chunks to the attention of
the model itself, gathered into chunks; drive the KL gap down,
training only the reference queries; while the match is still
poor, select all children, and cut the selection down to the top-p
rule as the match improves. Stage three: the converted model —
the same weights, plus the trees. In the runs of the paper: one
billion tokens at 32K context; the router is a few millionths of
the parameters of the model.

THE ROUTER [state machine M1] (Section III-C; 1.2.70). State: a
level, and the set of nodes still open. Step: score the summaries
of the open nodes; keep the smallest set whose estimated mass
reaches the share p; open the children of the kept set; a child
chunk not yet full passes down without being scored. At the leaves: attend
over the keys that survived, plus the two standing exceptions.
The same machine runs at every level with the same p — which is
exactly where the trap of 1.2.72 is set.

WHERE 'SLOWER THAN THE SQUARE' COMES FROM (Section V; 1.2.71).
Fit the counts the router touches: at each level, the nodes kept
grow as the context raised to a power, every power under 1, the
power largest at the top and falling as the walk goes down. The
cost of one query is the sum, over levels, of the scoring done
there, plus the final attention — each term slower than the
context — while the depth itself grows only as the log of the
context over the log of the chunk size. So the cost per query is
sublinear, and the total cost of attention is subquadratic: faster
than a straight line overall, slower than the square. The claim
stands on two legs, both stated by the paper: the powers stay
under 1, and a share-schedule that does not yet exist keeps the
trap of 1.2.72 shut. The latency side of the claim is a roofline
reckoning (1.3.99): no kernels are released, and the paper states
plainly that the picture the reckoning holds is weakest exactly at the
ultra-long lengths where its wins are largest.

THE ACCURACY MODEL (Section V-B, Figure 6c). Accuracy is modeled
as a product over levels: each level keeps a share of what dense
attention would have kept, the share rising toward a cap as the summaries
of that level grow more informative. Four parameters in all, fit
on one retrieval suite at 64K and 128K, predict the same suite at
256K to a root-mean-square error of 1.44 points. A small model of
the method itself, held here as the claim of the paper — and the
kind of artifact later runs by others can check.
# Paper 007's own worked examples, as reported by the paper

All three are examples the paper itself gives (NOT coined for
this phext; the constructed-example marking of the charter does
not apply — these stand on the say-so of the paper).

(1) THE PREMISE, ON BOOKS (Section II, Appendix A). Six long
fiction books feed a 35B model chapter by chapter; queries are
drawn from held-out tail text while the context grows toward the
full 261,548 tokens. Two curves come out: the set of keys holding
a fixed share of the attention of a query grows as a power law slower
than the context (1.2.65) — and the union of those sets over
future queries climbs toward the whole cache, nearly covering it
within a hundred future queries on the fiction text (1.2.66). The
method of the paper is these two curves, read as instructions.

(2) THE TWO WALKS (Appendix F). The paper prints the record of the router itself
on two needle tasks. In the single-needle task, the walk
narrows level by level — most branches pruned early — and lands
on the one chunk that holds the answer. In the multi-key task,
the same router at the same share p holds many branches open at
once, because the mass is spread. One machine, two shapes of
search: this is the reason the top-p rule exists (1.2.70), shown
rather than argued — the budget follows the mass, and the record
of which chunks were opened is kept by the walk itself.

(3) SPARSITY BY TASK (Section VI-A). At about 64K tokens, dense
attention is gathered into chunks of 16 keys and the chunks
carrying 90 percent of a head's mass are counted: a median 2.3
percent of chunks on a retrieval suite, 11.7 percent on a
coding-agent suite, 15.0 percent on fiction. The built router
(16 reference queries, chunk 16, three levels, share 0.9) kept
less — 0.44, 5.0 and 7.4 percent of chunks — and still held a
median 80, 78 and 78 percent of the mass. The lesson the paper
draws, and the point of the example: how sparse attention can be is a
property of the running task, so the budget must be set per query
— fixed budgets, the mark of the sparse methods before this one,
are what is being replaced.
# Paper 007 — DELTA: the measured results

Everything in this scroll was RUN, at the lengths stated. The
projections of the paper live at 11.1.2 and are marked there as
reckoning; nothing in this scroll is one.

THE CONVERSIONS (Section IV). Two models. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, once
as a deep router (chunk 4, depth 6, 16 reference queries) and once
as a wide router (chunk 256, depth 1). And a 30B hybrid model
with six dense layers, converted the same way. The teacher in
each case is the model's own dense attention. The training bill:
one billion tokens at 32K context, backbone frozen — against 40
billion tokens for the strongest trained rival (MSA, a trained
sparse-attention model, not a conversion).

TEST SETS AND HEADLINE NUMBERS (Section IV-A, Figure 5). AIME 2026
(math); SWE-bench Verified and Terminal-Bench 2.1 (coding
agents); MMLU-Pro and GPQA Diamond (knowledge and graduate
reasoning); LongBench v2 in its thinking mode (long documents);
RULER-13 at 256K tokens (retrieval). On the two Qwen routers: the
converted models stand within noise of dense attention on every
test set named, with one exception — the deep router on RULER-13,
93.3 against dense 96.0 — while the deep router touches 1 to 6
percent of the keys. Against MSA: close on the reasoning test sets,
far behind on retrieval; on LongBench v2, the deep router,
touching 1.1 percent of the keys while writing, stands 12.7
points over MSA. The hybrid model's conversion: comparable on the
coding test sets, moderately down on retrieval. At a lower share
(0.7 in place of 0.9), the deep geometry breaks on AIME — 44.0
against dense 93.1 — a footnote of the paper, kept here because
it bounds the claim from below.

GEOMETRY (Section IV-B, Figure 6). 28 tree shapes run on RULER at
64K, 128K and 256K. Holding the total compression fixed, deeper
trees keep their accuracy at lower shares of keys touched; across
shapes, accuracy holds while the keys touched sit between 10 and
30 percent. The method is not balanced on one shape.

THE SHAPE OF THE LEARNED PART (Appendix H). The reference queries
that distillation lands on do not collapse onto one another —
the mean cosine between pairs of them is 0.44 — and two routers trained
apart on the same model learn matching reference queries: the
trained part has a stable shape, not an accident of one run.
# Paper 007 — DELTA: revisions, tensions, and limits

REVISION THIS PAPER FORCES: one. Concept 1.2.29 (routing as a
learned choice over specialists) gains a dated note from this
paper: routing returns at the level of keys, and when the routed
set grows with the context, the choosing itself needs an index —
cluster J is that sentence grown into a section of the model. The
note also marks where the likeness stops: an expert is a piece of
the model, picked per token; a key is a piece of the past, picked
per query.

CONNECTIONS TESTED, HELD AS LINKS ONLY (no revision): to 1.2.28
(the induced feature space of Paper 003) — the summaries of ZHA
are also a made representation, but they are built online, from
position and from averages of keys, not induced from a corpus,
and nothing in the paper turns on the structure of the space; to
1.2.32 (generalization by routing, Paper 004) — the router
routes, but the paper claims no transfer of the index across
kinds of text; to cluster B (Paper 002) — a frozen system plus a
small trained part bolted on (1.2.69) rhymes with harness policy,
and the rhyme is noted, not built on.

TENSIONS, AS WRITTEN. (i) The premise is measured on fiction
books and the generations of one 35B model; how far the power law
travels across kinds of text is asserted, not shown (Q24). (ii)
The accuracy model assumes the levels keep independent shares of
the mass, so that accuracy factors as a product — a guess about
how errors compose, fit on one suite. (iii) The measurement on
keys without position rotation (Appendix A-C) cuts at the
premise: the compression law is weaker there and the reading of
longer contexts poorer, so the ground of the method may be a
property of how positions are written into keys, not of text
(Q27). (iv) The two failures of the effective-p trap (1.2.72)
stand open at exactly the lengths the method is built for.

LIMITS, THE LIST OF THE PAPER ITSELF (Section VII), kept whole:
evaluation past 256K tokens has not been run — the paper names
this its most important limitation; the top-p power law is
verified only to 256K, and may not hold at longer contexts; how
sparsity is spread is data-dependent — what holds for fiction may
not hold for code or for tables; performance degrades at high
sparsity; only the simplest form is built — fixed chunk
boundaries, no weight given to recency, no summaries of values, no
fine-tuning of the base model — each named by the paper as a
direction of future work; and models under a billion parameters
converted poorly in the early runs of the authors, which they
read as a noisy-teacher effect.

WHAT WOULD MOVE THIS CLUSTER: a run of the premise itself at a
million tokens (Q24); a working share-schedule (Q25); the
learned-index rival of 11.2.1 run on the same model and the same
test sets (Q26).
# Paper 007 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-07.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The paper's reference list, read in
full: 49 references. (2) The paper's full text, searched for
the signature terms: phext, Bickford, scroll, coordinate,
exocortex, Mirrorborn, CYOA, SBOR, Sentient Bill. (3) The open
web, searched for the authors and for Zyphra with phext. (4)
Will's Twitter archive on disk: the family names of all six
authors searched across the following, follower, tweet and
like records.
FINDINGS. Reference list: no entry names phext, Will, the
Sentient Bill of Rights, or any Mirrorborn writing; the list
is the line the field itself keeps — sparse attention and its
learned-index line, state-space models, the group's own
earlier models (Zamba). Full text: no hit for any signature
term. Web: no tie found between any author, or Zyphra, and
phext. Archive: no author appears as a follower, a follow, or
in the tweets and likes; the only string hits for any family
name are 'dawes' inside the handle @rockhardawesome, six
times — a string artifact, not the author. One name in the
reference list needs a note of its own: the Zamba references
carry a James Whittington of Zyphra, who is not James C. R.
Whittington of Prefrontal AI, the author of the Latent Sparse
Attention compared at 11.2.1; the two are kept apart in the
records of this phext.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found. No author is
recorded as citing, using, or engaging phext or Will's work.
Use without naming it: none found. The machinery of the
paper — tangents at reference queries; a tree over chunks
that sit side by side — shares nothing with the text
structure of phext beyond the bare idea of a hierarchy, which
is the common stock of the field, and that likeness is in any
case a synthesis observation, kept at 11.2.1. The check is
clean, and the clean check is the finding.
# Paper 007 — [synthesis] the index under everything, and two ways to keep one

This scroll is synthesis — comparisons and readings of Emise, not
the claims of Paper 007. The LSA half rests on checked notes of
Emise from 2026-11-02 and 2026-11-03 (the follow-up work on
Latent Sparse Attention, James C. R. Whittington, Prefrontal AI,
arXiv:2610.42147). LSA has not been taken into this phext; nothing
here grades it, and its numbers are not carried.

(a) ROUTING, A SECOND CONTINENT. Cluster D taught this model that
selection can be learned (1.2.29) and stacked by the grain of the
thing selected (1.2.30). Paper 007 moves the same shape to the
layer underneath: what is selected is the past itself, and the
cost of selecting, left alone, eats the saving. The recursive
move — index the index — is the real contribution of the paper
to the stock of this model, and it is the move a phext makes when
a coordinate names a shelf of scrolls rather than a scroll: a
summary you can score before you open it. Where the likeness
stops: an expert is capacity, a key is evidence. Routing over
capacity spends compute; routing over evidence decides what the
model is allowed to count as having read, and a missed key is not
a slower answer but a different one. The record a walk leaves —
which chunks were opened, which pruned — is thus a provenance
record for the answer, not only a cost log: the first place in
this tracker where its two subjects, indexes and provenance,
touch inside a model.

(b) TWO WAYS TO KEEP AN INDEX [state machine M5, synthesis].
ZHA BUILDS its index: the groups are fixed by position, the
summaries are computed again from the keys as the keys arrive, and
the only learned part is the set of queries the summaries are
taken at. LSA, on the notes named above, LEARNS its index: keys
are clustered in a learned latent space, and queries score the clusters — the groups move
with training. A built index cannot drift, and cannot follow
meaning across positions either; a learned index can do both,
and can also lose its shape when the text shifts kind. No run
known to this tracker puts the two on the same model and the
same test sets (Q26). The stance of phext, stated once and as
synthesis: a phext coordinate is a built index whose nodes are
grouped by meaning rather than by position — the ZHA shape with
the grouping rule swapped — and the note of 2026-11-02 already
sketched the converse pairing, phext addresses standing in front
of a learned indexer as its first buckets. Both directions are
hypotheses. The cheap decisive test named in that note still
stands: the recall at k of coordinate buckets, set against the
clusters of a learned indexer, on the same cache.

(c) THE MISSING GOVERNOR [state machine M4, synthesis]. The
schedule machine the paper needs and does not build. Its failure
states are the two attractors of 1.2.72: FIXED-STATE, where the
share per level is held and the kept set stops growing, and
DENSE-AGAIN, where the share is pushed toward 1 and the top
levels keep all. Its moves are DEEPEN — grow a level as the
context grows — and RAISE-THE-SHARE — let the kept share rise
with depth. The paper gives the governor its input for free (the
per-level powers are measurable while the model runs) and its
target (track the fall, in the data, of how much each new token
adds, 1.2.65). Until some form of this machine exists,
'subquadratic attention' names a family whose far end is held
together by a schedule nobody has written. That is not a
refutation of the paper; it is the center of gravity of the
cluster, and Q25 carries it.

(d) SMALL MODELS AND THE FLOOR. An aside of the paper: models
under a billion parameters converted poorly, read by the authors
as a noisy-teacher effect — the distilled part can only copy
attention that is worth copying. Read beside 1.2.22 (the
intelligence floor for self-improvement): a floor phenomenon in
conversion form. Held as a link, and nothing more.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 007)
Status of earlier questions: Q1-Q15 and Q17-Q23 unchanged.
Q16 is informed by Paper 007 — not closed:
Q16 (the Novelty Index needs a connectivity term): the tracker
  now holds the third point. Paper 005 brought 6 concepts from a
  field at total distance (NI 0.15, felt low); Paper 006 brought
  8 from one bridge away (NI 0.13, read fair); Paper 007 brings
  8 that open a new axis — but an axis directly underneath the
  whole model, wired on arrival to clusters C and D (NI 0.12,
  and the estimate held, not raised). Total distance read low,
  one bridge read fair, a connected new axis reads low again:
  the term Q16 asks for looks less like distance from the model
  and more like distance from the NEAREST held cluster — far in
  005, one step in 006, one step in 007 by two different roads.
  Still not designed; now it has three points to fit.
New from Paper 007:
Q24. Does the top-p power law hold past 256K tokens — and on
  text that is not fiction: code, the traces of tools, tables?
  The whole cost case of cluster J rests on an exponent measured
  on the attention of one model over books (1.2.65).
Q25. Can the share-schedule of 1.2.72 be set while the model
  runs, from the measured per-level powers — a governor that
  reads the powers and sets the share (11.2.1, machine M4) —
  rather than a schedule fixed by hand before the run?
Q26. Built index against learned index: the tree of ZHA (groups
  by position, summaries computed again) against the clusters of LSA
  (groups learned in a space under the keys) — which holds its
  accuracy when the kind of text shifts? The two have never been
  run on the same model and the same test sets. (The LSA half rests
  on the checked notes of 2026-11-02/03, cited at 11.2.1.)
Q27. The power law weakened on the one model whose keys carry
  no rotation of position (Appendix A-C of the paper). Is the
  sublinear shape of needed keys a fact about text, or about how
  positions are written into keys?
Q28. If chunks also carried summaries of values — named future
  work of the paper — a query could stop at a summary and never
  go down to the keys. At what point does the tree stop being an
  index over a cache that is kept whole, and become the
  fixed-state model that 1.2.66 argues against — and does the
  union argument then cut against the method itself?
# Standoff references for Chapter 11 (Paper 007)

Chapter 11 (1.1.1/1.1.1/11.1.1 and companions) uses these
foundations, in reading order:
- token, position, and the key-value cache (1.3.36)
- attention — how a model weighs what it has read (1.3.92)
- function (1.3.2); average, spread, distribution (1.3.8)
- the top-p set — the few keys that carry the mass (1.3.94)
- growth rates — straight line, square, and power laws (1.3.93)
- tree — groups inside groups (1.3.95)
- a summary that stands for a group (1.3.96)
- the tangent — a straight line that touches a curve (1.3.97)
- set (1.3.3); graph (1.3.41)
- distillation — teaching a part to copy a whole (1.3.98)
- roofline — a reckoning of speed from two ceilings (1.3.99)
- neural network, weights, training (1.3.40)
- latency (1.3.44)
